// WoSX's GPU tree: FCPW's binary BVH over triangles -- the one tree FCPW
// has on the GPU (fcpw/gpu/bvh.slang; GPUScene::transferToGPU takes the
// non-vectorized Bvh and nothing else), and so the tree WoSX's GPU solver
// walks -- declared as a tree whose interior node bounds its two children,
// with the layout that holds it.
//
// FCPW's GPU traversal (bvh.slang, Bvh::findClosestPoint; the same order as
// the CPU's bvh.inl, processSubtreeForClosestPoint): pop a node, skip it if
// its box is now farther than the best, test a leaf's triangles one by one,
// and at an interior node test BOTH children's boxes -- the radius lowered
// to each box's far corner -- and push the farther, then the nearer. In the
// tree language that shape is a node bounding its children (`with AABB(lo,
// hi) on children`): the children are tested at the node, the schedule's
// sort key orders them by their distance, and the best is lowered to the
// farthest a child's box can be (apps/rtq's `closest`, and FCPW's
// enqueueNodes); a node that bounds itself (layouts/fcpwb.bonsai) can only
// be tested as it is entered, and the children go left then right with no
// key, which is why that layout's matching schedule is far behind FCPW.
//
// The node bytes depart from FCPW's here, and only they. FCPW's node is its
// own box (GPUBvhNode: pMin, pMax, nPrimitives, offset; 32 bytes) and its
// traversal reads the two children's boxes out of the two children's rows;
// the layout language has no field that reads another row (`Nodes[index +
// 1u].pMin`, the extension PLAN.md put to the user on 2026-10-09), so the
// driver (fcpw_bvh2.h) writes each interior node's children's boxes into
// the node itself -- the two boxes component by component, two floats
// each, as FCPW's own four-wide MbvhNode holds its four -- then the two
// children's rows: 56 bytes, read as one row where FCPW reads three rows of
// 32. The same tree: FCPW's build, its splits, its leaves, its primitive
// order. A leaf row holds, as FCPW's Mbvh leaf rows do, a negative first
// slot that says which triangle the leaf starts at and a count in the
// second. The triangles are the program's Triangle elements in a plain
// array, in the order FCPW's build left its primitives, copied out of the
// polygon soup once as layouts/fcpwb.bonsai copies them (FCPW's GPU
// triangle is its three corners and index, 40 bytes, one row a triangle;
// the layout language's rows of elements come in tiles of more than one,
// so a row of one triangle with the edges derived from its corners is not
// a layout it can say, and the element array -- the corner, the two edges,
// the two ids, 44 bytes -- stands in for it).
schedule {
    tree[[Triangle]] BVH2 =
    | Interior(children : array[BVH2, 2], lo hi : vector[vec3f, 2])
        with AABB(lo, hi) on children
    | Leaf(data : array[Triangle]) with data = data;

    triangles : BVH2;

    layout triangles {
        // The primitives in the order FCPW's build left them
        // (Bvh::primitives, which a leaf's offset indexes), one Triangle
        // element each, filled by the driver from FCPW's soup.
        pCount : u32;
        prims : array[Triangle, pCount];
        // The nodes, the root at row 0, each interior row holding its two
        // children's boxes and rows (the left child is FCPW's next row, the
        // right its `offset` rows on); a leaf row `child0 = -(first + 1)`,
        // `child1` the count, as FCPW's Mbvh writes its leaf rows.
        nNodes : u32;
        group[nNodes] index : u32 {
            boxMin_x : vector[f32, 2];
            boxMin_y : vector[f32, 2];
            boxMin_z : vector[f32, 2];
            boxMax_x : vector[f32, 2];
            boxMax_y : vector[f32, 2];
            boxMax_z : vector[f32, 2];
            child0 : i32;
            child1 : i32;
            kind = child0[31:31];
            switch kind {
                0 => Interior {
                    children = vector[u32, 2]{cast[[u32]](child0), cast[[u32]](child1)};
                    lo = vector[vec3f, 2]{boxMin_x, boxMin_y, boxMin_z};
                    hi = vector[vec3f, 2]{boxMax_x, boxMax_y, boxMax_z};
                };
                _ => Leaf {
                    data = range(prims, cast[[u32]](0 - child0 - 1), cast[[u32]](child1));
                };
            };
        };
    };
}
